[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-32569"] :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-106836/selection/3dcd4fcb94fed378a4f5e04ab5390b616ec8c4723719e766023aa0e5214ed7a0" :answer/id #uuid "7592a942-792a-5caa-9389-c03d94a7e772" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106836/selection/4dc5fc38ce95b8325b18cb4b954489e4dd310cad641a0ef2d16920846b6990eb" :answer/id #uuid "28bfd293-a1a8-52a3-a5e5-9ffd2ca435a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106836/selection/f239b090dd6311856353f5e7a5a5491d44a85a6c5f96e71f7c81d40f60aacc49" :answer/id #uuid "b35d40ce-8638-5109-b727-607b6c6fe7b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106836/selection/d3530ebb68e055118542428a9512da335c03cc72f8367584d32b04bab9de4219" :answer/id #uuid "d0b9df09-ac34-5091-b157-310737b8e94a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106836/selection/22fa683b8df46e457d860041b5432d2b3a7862caafcbc862c1d0a297e081f9ae" :answer/id #uuid "b4b25255-0e2d-55ac-8710-1f57a35ec1c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106836/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e907506d-8508-57fb-9ed9-667447ee24db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106836/selection/3dcd4fcb94fed378a4f5e04ab5390b616ec8c4723719e766023aa0e5214ed7a0" :answer-field/choices ["q-106836/selection/3dcd4fcb94fed378a4f5e04ab5390b616ec8c4723719e766023aa0e5214ed7a0" "q-106836/selection/4dc5fc38ce95b8325b18cb4b954489e4dd310cad641a0ef2d16920846b6990eb" "q-106836/selection/f239b090dd6311856353f5e7a5a5491d44a85a6c5f96e71f7c81d40f60aacc49" "q-106836/selection/d3530ebb68e055118542428a9512da335c03cc72f8367584d32b04bab9de4219" "q-106836/selection/22fa683b8df46e457d860041b5432d2b3a7862caafcbc862c1d0a297e081f9ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106836" :question/id #uuid "103c5b53-bb22-5ad8-9493-a7215f314160" :question/math-academy-id "q-106836" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-106836/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106836"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106842/selection/e11083596982df39e9528716029dc8ee0e87e90269861e6c404967f831c9315c" :answer/id #uuid "7c0b8c47-a4e4-56c3-9436-77fd09a67f26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106842/selection/b432627a7425a619f5ffa37d63f966b5961e3d5485d6c7b919a0e06540645670" :answer/id #uuid "3e418a10-8ea8-560e-b987-98500394f6d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106842/selection/6deaf